course: books
lesson: 23
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter XII, part 1: Alice's Evidence (Rule Forty-two and the unsigned letter)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: «Here!» cries Alice, and jumps up so fast — forgetting how big she has grown — that her skirt knocks over the jury-box and all the jurors tumble onto the crowd. It reminds her of the goldfish bowl she upset last week, and she quickly scoops them back in, so quickly that she puts Bill the Lizard in upside down, waving his tail.
explain: The King asks what she knows about the tarts. «Nothing.» «Nothing whatever?» «Nothing whatever.» The King calls that «very important», the White Rabbit hints he means «unimportant», and the King tries both words to see which sounds better. The jury writes down some of each. Alice thinks it doesn't matter a bit.
explain: Then the King reads a rule from his note-book: «Rule Forty-two. All persons more than a mile high to leave the court.» Everyone looks at Alice. She is not a mile high, she says, and anyway he just made that rule up. «It's the oldest rule in the book,» says the King. «Then it ought to be Number One,» says Alice — and the King turns pale and shuts his book.
explain: The White Rabbit produces new evidence: a paper with no address, not a letter but a set of verses, not in the Knave's handwriting and not signed. The King decides that the Knave must have imitated someone's handwriting, and that not signing it proves he is up to mischief. The court claps; the Queen says it proves he is guilty.
explain: Alice is no longer afraid. «It proves nothing of the sort!» she says: «you don't even know what they're about!» So the King orders the verses read, with famous advice: «Begin at the beginning, and go on till you come to the end: then stop.»
fact: Alice jumps up «in such a hurry that she tipped over the jury-box with the edge of her skirt», upsetting all the jurymen onto the heads of the crowd.
fact: It reminds her of «a globe of goldfish she had accidentally upset the week before»; she feels they must be put back at once, or they would die.
fact: The King: «The trial cannot proceed until all the jurymen are back in their proper places—all», looking hard at Alice.
fact: Alice puts the Lizard in head downwards; she sets him right, though she thinks he'd be «quite as much use in the trial one way up as the other».
fact: The jury writes «a history of the accident»; Bill is too shocked to do anything but sit «with its mouth open, gazing up into the roof of the court».
fact: «What do you know about this business?» — «Nothing.» — «Nothing whatever?» — «Nothing whatever.» The King says «That's very important».
fact: The White Rabbit corrects him: «Unimportant, your Majesty means, of course», frowning and making faces; the King mutters «important—unimportant—» as if trying which sounds best.
fact: Some jurors write «important» and some «unimportant»; Alice thinks «it doesn't matter a bit».
fact: «Rule Forty-two. All persons more than a mile high to leave the court.» Alice: «I'm not a mile high.» King: «You are.» Queen: «Nearly two miles high.»
fact: Alice: «that's not a regular rule: you invented it just now.» King: «It's the oldest rule in the book.» Alice: «Then it ought to be Number One.» The King turns pale, shuts his note-book and says to the jury, in a low, trembling voice, «Consider your verdict» — the second time he has said it at this trial.
fact: The White Rabbit thinks the paper is a letter «written by the prisoner to—to somebody», and the King says «It must have been that, unless it was written to nobody, which isn't usual».
fact: The new paper «isn't directed at all» (directed = addressed), and it is «a set of verses», not in the prisoner's handwriting.
fact: The King says the Knave «must have imitated somebody else's hand» (hand = handwriting).
fact: The Knave: «I didn't write it, and they can't prove I did: there's no name signed at the end.» King: «If you didn't sign it, that only makes the matter worse. You must have meant some mischief, or else you'd have signed your name like an honest man.»
fact: Alice has been growing all through the trial and gets bolder as she gets bigger; the Queen says «That proves his guilt»; Alice says «It proves nothing of the sort! Why, you don't even know what they're about!»
fact: The King: «Begin at the beginning, and go on till you come to the end: then stop.»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «canny» (North East England) = quite, or nice; «howay» (North East) = come on; «sound» (Liverpool) = fine, decent; «no cap» = honestly; «ate» = did it brilliantly.
exercise: What does Alice cry when her name is called? | «Here!» | «Present, your Majesty, if it please you» | «Who, me?» | «Nonsense!»
exercise: Why does Alice knock over the jury-box? | She forgets how large she has grown and jumps up in a hurry | She is angry with the jury for writing down «stupid things» and kicks it over on purpose | The Queen pushes her | She trips over Bill
exercise: What does the scene remind Alice of? | A globe of goldfish she upset the week before | The pool of tears she cried in the long hall when she was nine feet high | A pack of cards falling | The Mock Turtle's school
exercise: Why does Alice rush to put the jurors back? | She has a vague idea they must go back at once, or they would die, like the goldfish | The King tells her she will be beheaded at once if she doesn't hurry | She wants the trial to end quickly so she can eat the tarts | She likes tidying
exercise: «The trial cannot proceed until all the jurymen are back.» How does the King say «all»? | With great emphasis, looking hard at Alice | In a soft whisper, to the Queen alone, so that nobody else could hear | Singing it | Laughing
exercise: What mistake does Alice make with Bill the Lizard? | She puts him in head downwards | She puts him in the wrong box, next to the Queen's tarts | She drops him hard on the floor | She forgets all about poor Bill
exercise: What does Alice think about Bill being upside down? | He'd be quite as much use one way up as the other | It must hurt him terribly, and she should say sorry to him | It is very rude to the court | It's the Queen's fault
exercise: What do the jurors write after the accident? | A history of the accident | Their own names again, in case they forgot | The final verdict of the trial | «Stupid things» all over again
exercise: What does Bill do instead of writing? | Sits with his mouth open, gazing up at the roof | Cries and hunts all over the court for his squeaky pencil | Writes with his tail | Falls asleep
exercise: «What do you know about this business?» What does Alice answer? | «Nothing» | «Everything» | «The Knave did it» | «Ask the Hatter»
exercise: What does the King say about Alice's answer «Nothing whatever»? | «That's very important» | «That's very rude, child» | «Off with her head at once» | «Write that down twice, jurors»
exercise: Who corrects the King, and how? | The White Rabbit: «Unimportant, your Majesty means» | The Queen, by stamping hard on his foot under the throne | Alice | The Knave
exercise: What does the King do with the words «important» and «unimportant»? | Tries them over, as if to see which sounds best | Writes them on a slate | Asks the jury to vote on which word is the right one | Forbids them
exercise: What do the jurors write down? | Some «important», some «unimportant» | All of them write «important», in big letters | All «unimportant» | Nothing
exercise: What is Rule Forty-two? | All persons more than a mile high to leave the court | All persons must stand up when the King speaks to them | No tarts in court | No talking
exercise: How high is Alice, according to the Queen? | Nearly two miles high | Exactly one mile high, no more | Just over nine feet high | Only three inches high
exercise: What does Alice say about Rule Forty-two? | It's not a regular rule: he invented it just now | It's a very good rule, and she will obey it at once and leave | She has read it before | It's the oldest rule
exercise: «It's the oldest rule in the book.» How does Alice answer? | «Then it ought to be Number One» | «Then I'll go» | «Prove it» | «What book? Show me this book of yours, please»
exercise: What does the King do after Alice's answer? | Turns pale and shuts his note-book | Shouts «Off with her head!» and calls the executioner | Laughs | Gives up the trial
exercise: What new evidence does the White Rabbit bring? | A paper that has just been picked up | The stolen tarts | The Knave's crown | The cook's pepper-box, left behind on the floor
exercise: Who is the paper «directed to»? | Nobody: nothing is written on the outside | The Queen | The Duchess, in prison | The King
exercise: What is the paper, when the Rabbit opens it? | A set of verses | A letter to the Queen | A list of singers | A recipe for tarts
exercise: The verses are not in the Knave's handwriting. What does the King conclude? | He must have imitated somebody else's hand | He must be innocent | Someone else must have stolen the tarts and written it | The Queen wrote it
exercise: Why does the Knave say they can't prove he wrote it? | There is no name signed at the end | He can't write at all, not even his name | He was locked in chains the whole time | The White Rabbit must have written it
exercise: How does the King turn «not signed» against the Knave? | An honest man would have signed his name, so he meant mischief | Only kings and queens may sign their names on papers in court | Unsigned papers are always written by Knaves | It isn't usual
exercise: What advice does the King give the White Rabbit about reading? | «Begin at the beginning, and go on till you come to the end: then stop» | «Start at the end and work back to the beginning, slowly» | «Read only the best bits, and skip the long verses in the middle» | «Sing it»
test: Grace from Newcastle: «howay, Alice was tiny when she jumped up.» (howay = come on) Put it right. | She had grown very large, so her skirt knocked over the jury-box | Right: she was three inches tall and fell off her chair | She was her normal size, about as tall as her sister | She was invisible
test: Why does Alice feel the jurors «must be collected at once and put back»? | The goldfish she upset kept running in her head, and fish die out of water | The King ordered her to, with a frown | She wanted to win | They were crying
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Bill the Lizard writes a full history of the accident.» | False: he just sits with his mouth open, gazing at the roof | True: he writes the longest history of all, using his tail for a pencil | True, with his tail | False: he leaves the court
test: Sam: «Alice said she knew loads about the tarts, no cap.» (no cap = honestly) Put it right. | She said «Nothing» — «Nothing whatever» | Right: she saw the Knave take them on a summer day and run off | She said the Hatter took them | She said nothing at all, not a word
test: «Important — unimportant — unimportant — important.» What does this show about the King? | He cares how a word sounds more than what it means | He is very careful about the truth of each word he says to the jury | He can't speak English | He is reading a poem
test: Why does it «not matter a bit» to Alice what the jury writes? | Their notes are nonsense anyway | She knows the King will win in the end | She can't read their slates from where she is sitting | She is leaving the court very soon
test: Why can't Rule Forty-two be «the oldest rule in the book»? | The oldest rule would be Number One, not Forty-two | Books of rules never have more than ten rules in them | It is printed in red | The Queen wrote it
test: Ella, a bit salty (cross): «the King proved Alice was a mile high.» Put it right. | He only said «You are»; the Queen said «Nearly two miles» — no proof at all | Right: he measured her with a very long ruler and wrote it down | Alice agreed she was | The jury measured her
test: What would happen if anyone could «win» an argument by inventing a rule just then? | Whoever is in charge could make any rule to get rid of anyone | Trials would be fairer and quicker than ever before | Nobody would ever need to leave | The rules would all be Number One
test: «It must have been that, unless it was written to nobody, which isn't usual.» What is silly about the King's reasoning? | He says a letter is written to somebody, which tells us nothing new | He proves very cleverly who the letter is written to | He reads the letter first | He is right
test: The verses aren't in the Knave's handwriting, «and that's the queerest thing». How does the King twist this into proof? | He says the Knave must have copied someone else's handwriting | He says it proves the Knave can't write a single word | He says the Queen wrote them | He gives up
test: «If you didn't sign it, that only makes the matter worse.» Why is this unfair to the Knave? | Whatever he does — sign or not sign — the King calls it proof of guilt | The King is quite right, and that is a fair test that any judge would use | The Knave did sign it | The Knave is the King's son
test: Alice says «It proves nothing of the sort!» What gives her the courage now? | She has been growing, and she sees the reasoning is nonsense | The Queen asked her opinion very politely | The Gryphon pushed her | She is very small and hidden behind the Gryphon's wing
test: Looking back: earlier in the book, the Mock Turtle kept Alice waiting. What did Alice think then that fits the King's «Begin at the beginning»? | «I don't see how he can ever finish, if he doesn't begin» | «Off with his head» | «Curiouser and curiouser!» in the long hall | «Who are you?»
test: Looking back: when did Alice first stand up to the Queen, and what did she say? | At the croquet-ground, she said «Nonsense!» to «Off with her head!» | At the tea-party, she said «No room!» | In the kitchen, she said «Pig!» | At the very start of the trial, she said «Stupid things!» to the jurors
test: Looking back: in chapter I, Alice remembered «simple rules» before drinking. What was one? | If you drink much from a bottle marked «poison», it will disagree with you | All persons more than a mile high must leave | Never eat mushrooms found growing in a wood | Always bow to queens
test: Looking back: what did the jurors do in the first part of the trial when Alice said «Stupid things!»? | Wrote «stupid things!» on their slates | Gave Alice a prize and a round of applause | Left the court | Sang a song
test: ★★★ A strange letter: «not directed», «not in the prisoner's handwriting», «no name signed». How many of these three facts are real evidence that the Knave wrote it? | None of them | One of them, the missing name, because honest men sign their names | Two of them | All three, as the King says
test: ★★★ The King said «Consider your verdict» first after the accusation, and again after Rule Forty-two. Counting both, how many times has he said it so far in this lesson and the last two? | 2 | 1 | 3 | 20
test: ★★★ «Nothing» — «Nothing whatever» — «That's very important.» If Alice knows nothing, what is her evidence worth to the trial? | Nothing: it is unimportant, as the Rabbit says | Everything: it is the most important evidence in the whole trial | Half of the evidence | It proves the Knave stole them
exam: Identify the occasion of the overturning of the jury-box. | Alice's rising in haste, unmindful of her increased size | An assault upon the jury by the Queen with her inkstand and her sceptre | The collapse of the Lizard | A sudden gust of wind
exam: It is given that the King cited «Rule Forty-two». Select the response which most accurately characterises Alice's objection thereto. | That it was not a regular rule but had been invented at that moment | That she was less than a mile high and therefore exempt | That rules of court are not binding upon visitors from beyond the kingdom | That the rule had been repealed
exam: Ascertain the inference drawn by the King from the absence of a signature upon the verses. | That the Knave must have intended mischief | That the Knave was innocent and should be released forthwith | That the verses were written by the Queen | That the verses were of no importance
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the King's instruction for the reading of the verses? | Begin at the beginning, proceed to the end, and then stop | Commence at the end and proceed backwards, verse by verse | Omit the second verse | Read only the signature
